.TH SSE 3
.SH NAME
sse \- server-sent events parser
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <sse.h>
.P
.B #include <sse.h>
.br
.B typedef struct { ... } sse;
.br
.B typedef struct { const char *event, *data; size_t data_len; const char* id; long retry; } sse_event;
.br
.B typedef void sse_callback(const sse_event*, void*);
.SH DESCRIPTION
Server-sent events (media type text/event-stream) are what APIs answer with when a
request asks to stream, for example the chat APIs of language models. The stream is text in lines;
.B data: ...
lines carry the payload, an empty line ends an event:
.P
.nf
: comment, often a keep-alive
event: message_start
data: {"a":1}

data: line one
data: line two

.fi
.P
The parser takes the bytes of the stream in any pieces, cut anywhere, and calls a function for every
event that is complete. It handles LF, CR LF and CR line ends (also when the CR and LF come in
different pieces), a byte order mark at the start, comments, and the fields
.BR data ,
.BR event ,
.B id
and
.BR retry ;
other fields are ignored. Several data lines of an event are joined by newlines. An event without
data lines is not dispatched. An event that is cut off by the end of the stream is never dispatched.
.P
The fields of
.B sse_event
are valid during the callback only:
.TP
.I event
the event type, "message" if the stream gave none.
.TP
.I data, data_len
the data, also terminated by a NUL.
.TP
.I id
the last event id (it stays set for the following events), empty if there was none.
.TP
.I retry
the reconnection time in milliseconds that the server asked for, -1 if it did not.
.P
Many APIs end the stream with the data "[DONE]"; that is up to the caller to recognize.
.SH SEE ALSO
sse_init(3), sse_feed(3), sse_free(3), http_recv(3)
